  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>Pattison's Terrace (also printed Pattison Terrace) is a row of houses in <ref target="civic?id=Leicester%20Street">Leicester Street</ref>, near the <ref target="civic?id=Pelham%20Street">Pelham Street</ref> corner. It is listed as a corner in the Leicester Street listings from 1870 to 1895, and as its own street from 1874 to 1885, with five houses numbered 1 to 5. In 1877 the directory lists a music teacher at No. 1, and in 1879 a bootmaker at No. 4.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>In 1874 the terrace's listing runs on past a right-of-way to the <ref target="civic?id=Corkman%20Hotel">Carlton Inn</ref> at the Pelham Street corner, under John Cozens, and the inn follows the terrace again in 1877 and 1878.</p>
      </div>
